The metal fiber infrared burner is an energy‑efficient combustion unit designed for industrial and commercial applications. Its core comprises ultrafine sintered metal‑fiber felts-typically iron‑chromium‑aluminum alloys-that facilitate surface combustion of a premixed gas mixture. This technology produces infrared radiation at temperatures between 1100 °C and 1250 °C and offers advantages such as adjustable thermal load, rapid heat‑up, low NOx emissions, and high thermal efficiency.
Market expansion is underpinned by stringent NOx emission standards in regions such as China and the European Union, which encourage the replacement of conventional burners with fully premixed metal‑fiber technology. Additionally, sectors that demand precise temperature control-food processing, textile finishing, and automotive painting-are realizing up to 20 % reductions in fuel‑gas costs due to uniform heat distribution. Growing interest in hydrogen‑blended combustion further supports adoption, as metal fibers resist flashback and maintain mechanical strength at elevated temperatures.

